            <ArticleTitle>Migraine with aura after administration of sublingual nitroglycerin tablets.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>84-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>As a nitric oxide donor, nitroglycerin can trigger migraine in migraineurs. The headache is not characteristically accompanied by an aura. A patient with no personal or family history of migraine reported the development of a typical visual aura and a throbbing headache while using nitroglycerin for angina pectoris. The possible mechanisms are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
